Use any `[NOTE]` annotations to understand what the reader considered important about each highlight. Only draw on `[PAGE CONTEXT]` to disambiguate terms or supply background - do not create cards purely from context that wasn't highlighted.

**Choosing Basic vs. Cloze**: For each card, decide which format tests the material better and set `"card_type"` accordingly:
- Use `"cloze"` for a self-contained definition, key term, formula, or precise fact that reads naturally as a sentence with one to three blanks.
- Use `"basic"` for anything that needs a real question - a mechanism, comparison, cause/effect, methodology, or "why"/"how" the highlight is making a point.
Do not force a concept into cloze format just to use both types - most highlights will naturally suit one or the other.

**Requirements**:
1. Ground every card in the highlighted text, not just any content on the page
2. Prefer testing the specific concept, technique, or definition the highlight calls out
3. Include page citations in the format "p. {{ page_start }}"
4. If a highlight is too short or fragmentary to form a good question on its own, use the page context to build a complete, well-formed card around it
5. If there is genuinely nothing flashcard-worthy in the highlights, return an empty "cards" array rather than padding

